# City Cleaner Coding Rules

## Core Style
- Prefer clean, simple, readable code over complex defensive logic.
- Optimize for obvious control flow and maintainability.
- Avoid unnecessary abstraction and over-engineering.

## Null Handling
- For required references/components, fail fast (do not silently ignore nulls).

## Edge Cases
- Handle practical gameplay cases only.
- Avoid adding extreme edge-case guards unless they solve a real issue seen in this project.

## Function Naming and Structure
- Prefer direct, action-oriented names (`SpawnDirtling`, `SetVulnerable`, `StartSpawning`).
- Avoid vague "Try..." method naming unless failure is genuinely expected and meaningful.

## Function Parameters Formatting
- Prefer function parameter lists on a single line when reasonably readable.
- Do not split parameters into one-per-line unless the signature is genuinely too long or harms readability.

## Loop Variable Naming
- Use clear, full variable names in loops.
- Do not over-shorten loop variables (e.g., prefer `infoText` over `t` in `foreach`).

## Gameplay Logic Preferences
- Use explicit state transitions (e.g., `StartSpawning` / `StopSpawning`) instead of scattered boolean checks.
- Keep damage and state logic straightforward and centralized.

## Error Visibility
- Prefer surfacing setup/configuration problems early (clear failures) instead of silent fallbacks.
